/Title (What it does: Replaces OS-level IOCTL calls with a custom signed kernel driver that communicates directly with the storage controller firmware. It triggers hardware-level crypto-scramble and block-erase commands \(NIST SP 800-88 "Purge" and IEEE 2883 "Sanitize"\).)
/Dest [15 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 549 0 R
/Next 702 0 R
>>
endobj
617 0 obj
<<
/Title (Why it stands out: Industry-standard tools issue proprietary PDF certificates that can be edited to fake an audit. A decentralized, on-chain Merkle proof provides mathematical, third-party verifiable proof that data was destroyed at a specific second.)
/Dest [15 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 549 0 R
/Prev 703 0 R
>>
endobj
618 0 obj
<<
/Title (What it does: Threat actors and malware hide files inside physical drive zones that the operating system cannot see: Host Protected Areas \(HPA\), Device Configuration Overlays \(DCO\), and unallocated volume slack. This module unmasks these hidden sectors before running recovery or sanitization.)
/Dest [16 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 550 0 R
/Next 704 0 R
>>
endobj
619 0 obj
<<
/Title (Why it stands out: Standard forensic recovery software only parses visible partition tables. If an attacker hid 10GB of exfiltrated data inside an HPA, standard tools report the drive as clean. Zero Trace identifies and extracts covert storage structures.)
/Dest [16 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 550 0 R
/Prev 705 0 R
>>
endobj
620 0 obj
<<
/Title (What it does: Traditional shredders overwrite the contents of a target file, but they leave filenames, timestamps, and cached copies in the NTFS $LogFile, $UsnJrnl, $MFT, and Volume Shadow Copies \(VSS\). The surgical eraser sanitizes the target file and hunts down every operational trace of that file across the file system metadata without corrupting adjacent files.)
/Dest [16 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 551 0 R
/Next 706 0 R
>>
endobj
621 0 obj
<<
/Title (Why it stands out: Existing tools either wipe the whole disk or leave forensic breadcrumbs across system journals. Surgical micro-sanitization provides true "anti-forensic clean erasure" for legal redaction cases where an agency needs to destroy specific files while keeping others intact.)
/Dest [16 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 551 0 R
/Prev 707 0 R
>>
